From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 05:09:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] AR escalation as a production toggle; record the project
 context that reframes it

Harness 279 -> 303 assertions, 0 failing. Implements the FW-creel-meeting
ladder as a run_config toggle, and records the context that changes how the
rest of this work should be read.

PROJECT CONTEXT, now at the top of run_config.R and CLAUDE.md
WDFW has published NO estimate from this pipeline; main is the state before
the FW creel meeting and before OSP confirmed they can supply boat counts.
There is no published figure a change has to stay consistent with, so
continuity with an earlier internal run is not on its own a reason to prefer
a modelling choice. The OSP stream is, per day, total returning vessels and
the fraction that were CRABBING ONLY, which excludes combo trips that also
crabbed: a LOWER BOUND on crabbing vessels, not the crabbing fraction f.
That is what osp_crab_lower / f_lower exist for; do not wire it in as f. Its
purpose is to improve the boat estimate's accuracy and reduce its
uncertainty; more boat interviews are needed next season regardless.

THE LADDER ALREADY EXISTED (ar_escalate). What it lacked now exists:
  * PER-POPULATION / PER-SUB-SEASON SCOPE. TRUE, c("shore"), or
    list(shore = "all_gear"). Every rung is a multi-hour fit and two
    components have known answers (shore pot closure funnelled at daily with
    1,165 divergences; the boat diverged on ~100% of iterations), so
    escalating those from the top burns known-bad fits.
  * EACH RUNG'S OWN ESTIMATE. ar_escalation_log.csv kept sampler diagnostics
    only; the estimate was discarded when `fit` was overwritten, so the one
    thing a ladder exists to show was the one thing not recorded. It now
    carries catch_median / lo95 / hi95 / pi_width / pi_width_rel /
    effort_median and a `selected` flag, and the HTML report tabulates them.
  * ar_escalate_stop = "first_pass" (default, byte-identical to the previous
    behaviour: the production rule) or "all_rungs" (the diagnostic mode that
    fits every rung in ONE render).

THE LIMIT, stated because it bounds the mechanism. The ladder escalates on
the CONVERGENCE gate. On the 2026-08-31 production run every component
passes it, shore all-gear at daily included, so the production rule applied
to this season changes nothing. That same fit carries p_loo 35.2% of n_obs,
41 Pareto k > 0.7 and coverage_50 0.701 against a nominal 0.500, all
invisible to the gate by construction. Making the ladder react to adequacy
is a change to what the gate tests, not a setting.

NARROWEST-INTERVAL SELECTION WAS TESTED AND IS NOT THE DEFAULT. On the boat
2x2 the two MISCALIBRATED cells have the narrowest RELATIVE intervals
(142.4%, 146.6% vs 153.7%, 156.0%), because a latent process absorbing
observation noise reports a tighter interval. On ABSOLUTE width the
calibrated cell wins, but catch and width are nearly proportional (ratio
0.64 in all four cells), so the criterion is close to "smallest estimate"
and would bias the harvest number down. "narrowest_pi" is available for
breaking a tie between rungs that are already adequate.

BATCH SIMPLIFICATION. run_shore_ar_zi_2026-09-03.R replaces its three forced
rungs with one run of the production toggle: 7 fits instead of 12, one
output folder, 17-20 h instead of 23-26 h, and it exercises the mechanism a
future season will actually use rather than an experiment-only ar_force.

Verified: harness 303/303; batch dry run (P1 PASS, all four stages resolve);
all three Stan models pass stanc; both drivers purl and parse; 38 helpers
parse; stage5 self-test 22/22.

